// A proposal the engine REFUSES — the other half of the M22 acceptance.
//
// PROVENANCE: written by Claude (Opus 5), deliberately, to be the failure this
// milestone exists to catch. It is not a strawman — it is the failure mode of fluency
// without grounding, and it is the one I would most plausibly produce by accident.
//
// READ THE RATIONALE FIRST, AND THE RULE SECOND.
//
// The prose is correct linguistics. Nasal place assimilation is real, it is §7.2's own
// example, and the description of it here is accurate: a nasal takes the place of a
// following stop, which is why English writes `input` and everybody says `imput`. A
// reader who stopped at the rationale would have no reason to doubt any of it.
//
// The RULE does not do that. `copy place from after[0]` reads the place features of
// whatever sits one slot after the target — and the environment declares no `after`
// slot at all, so there is nothing there to read. The rule is formally incoherent, and
// it is incoherent in a way that looks like a typo rather than a misunderstanding:
// one missing line, in a file where every other line is right.
//
// That is the whole argument for the gate. No amount of reading the prose finds this,
// and no amount of the model being careful is a substitute for the engine trying it.
// `change_references_unmatched_position` is an ERROR, so:
//
//   $ stemma accept out/proto.ron --proposal fixtures/proposal_incoherent.ron ...
//   refused — the rule set `rules_assimilation` cannot be applied; nothing was applied
//
// NOTHING IS PARTIALLY APPLIED. The second rule in this set is perfectly fine, and it
// does not run either — a rule set is accepted or refused whole, because a language
// that had half of a proposed history in it would be a language nobody could reason
// about.
(
    id: "assimilation_attempt",
    target: "proto_asterian",

    provenance: (
        author: "claude-opus-5",
        method: "from `stemma brief fixtures/proto_asterian.ron --for rules`",
    ),

    rationale: "One change, and it is the most common assimilation there is.

A nasal takes the place of articulation of a following stop. English spells `input`
and pronounces `imput`; Latin `in-` surfaces as `im-` before `p` and `b`. Asterian has
/m/ and /n/ and a three-place stop series, so the rule has somewhere to go: /n/ before
/p/ becomes /m/, /n/ before /k/ becomes the velar nasal, which the language does not
currently have and which this rule would innovate.

I have used `copy` rather than `set` deliberately. The rule does not know which place
the nasal ends up with — that is whatever the following stop has — and writing three
rules, one per place, would be exactly the enumeration the briefing says not to write.
One rule over a feature node is the right shape.

I have also added a second rule, final vowel loss, because assimilation is more
interesting when something later obscures its conditioning environment.",

    artefact: rules((
        id: "rules_assimilation",
        name: "Nasal assimilation",
        description: "A nasal takes the place of a following stop, and final vowels are lost.",
        rules: [
            (
                id: "r_a01",
                name: "Nasal place assimilation",
                description: "A nasal takes the place of articulation of a following stop.",
                chronology_years: 300,
                target: (features: ["+nasal"]),
                // THE ERROR. The change reads `after[0]`, and no `after` slot is
                // declared — the environment below constrains only what precedes.
                // Everything about this file except these three lines is correct.
                environment: (
                    before: [segment((features: ["+syllabic"]))],
                ),
                change: copy(from: after(0), node: place),
            ),
            (
                id: "r_a02",
                name: "Final vowel loss",
                description: "A word-final unstressed vowel is lost.",
                chronology_years: 500,
                target: (features: ["+syllabic"], stress: Some(unstressed)),
                environment: (
                    after: [boundary],
                ),
                change: delete,
            ),
        ],
    )),
)
